---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  name: "Sourcing Method",
  description:
    "How Philosophy Blind Box sources, verifies, and documents English philosophy quotations.",
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Sourcing Method | Philosophy Blind Box"
  description="How we source philosophy quotations: reference works, translation checks, attribution review, and documentation standards."
  canonical="/sourcing-method"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="page-shell">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <h1>Sourcing method</h1>
      <p>
        Every quotation in the archive should be traceable to a thinker, a tradition,
        and a documented English rendering.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose-layout">
      <aside class="prose-aside">
        <p>
          Method before virality. A memorable sentence without a responsible source does
          not belong here. The archive currently holds about 650–700 verified passages.
        </p>
      </aside>
      <article class="prose">
        <h2>Scope of the corpus</h2>
        <p>
          Sourcing covers Greco-Roman, Chinese, Indian, Islamic, Jewish, Japanese, and
          Africana traditions, with expanded attention to women philosophers. Each
          tradition is represented through citable English renderings rather than
          exhaustive inclusion.
        </p>

        <h2>Reference hierarchy</h2>
        <p>
          We prefer critical editions and widely used translations, then standard
          handbooks and encyclopedias such as the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy
          and the Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy for biographical and contextual
          checks, then reputable historical surveys. Social media and unsourced quote
          aggregators are not accepted as primary evidence.
        </p>

        <h2>Verification steps</h2>
        <p>
          For each candidate passage we ask: Who is the attributed author? In which work
          or tradition does the line appear? Is the English wording attested in a
          reliable translation? Does the sentence distort the surrounding argument when
          removed from context?
        </p>
        <p>
          Passages that fail attribution or distort meaning are rejected. Passages that
          survive are tagged with school, source, and theme labels for discovery.
        </p>

        <h2>Theme labels</h2>
        <p>
          Themes are editorial access points for readers, not claims that a historical
          author used the modern English category. Thin or duplicate theme labels are
          merged into stronger canonical themes so that theme pages remain useful rather
          than sparse.
        </p>

        <h2>Biographical notes</h2>
        <p>
          Thinker pages that include dates, works, and overviews follow mainstream
          scholarly consensus (for example, Confucius 551–479 BCE; Socrates c. 469–399
          BCE). Where historicity is debated—as with Laozi—we state the uncertainty
          plainly.
        </p>

        <h2>AI and editorial responsibility</h2>
        <p>
          Drafting tools may assist organization, but attributions, dates, and
          philosophical claims are checked against human-curated sources before
          publication. The archive’s credibility depends on that review.
        </p>

        <h2>Related</h2>
        <p>
          Read the <a href="/editorial-policy">editorial policy</a> and
          <a href="/about">about this archive</a>.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
